<!doctype html><html lang="en" dir="ltr" data-theme="kujo-light"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><link rel="icon" href="/assets/presentation/favicon.svg"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>KUJO — Trust at the speed of AI. — Read text</title><meta name="description" content="Read the complete presentation: 18-slide investor briefing with source evidence, recorded execution and editable planning assumptions."><link rel="canonical" href="https://invest.kujolang.ai/reading/"><meta property="og:type" content="website"><meta property="og:title" content="KUJO — Trust at the speed of AI. — Read text"><meta property="og:description" content="Read the complete presentation: 18-slide investor briefing with source evidence, recorded execution and editable planning assumptions."><meta name="twitter:title" content="KUJO — Trust at the speed of AI. — Read text"><meta name="twitter:description" content="Read the complete presentation: 18-slide investor briefing with source evidence, recorded execution and editable planning assumptions."><meta property="og:url" content="https://invest.kujolang.ai/reading/"><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage","description":"Read the complete presentation: 18-slide investor briefing with source evidence, recorded execution and editable planning assumptions.","inLanguage":"en","name":"KUJO — Trust at the speed of AI. — Read text","url":"https://invest.kujolang.ai/reading/"}</script><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/sitekit/sitekit.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/presentation/presentation.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/deck/theme.css"><meta property="og:image" content="https://invest.kujolang.ai/assets/deck/social/reading.00a62e21291e.png"><meta property="og:image:secure_url" content="https://invest.kujolang.ai/assets/deck/social/reading.00a62e21291e.png"><meta property="og:image:type" content="image/png"><meta property="og:image:width" content="1200"><meta property="og:image:height" content="630"><meta property="og:image:alt" content="KUJO — Read the briefing. The complete Kujo investor presentation."><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:image" content="https://invest.kujolang.ai/assets/deck/social/reading.00a62e21291e.png"><meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="KUJO — Read the briefing. The complete Kujo investor presentation."></head><body><a class="p-skip" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a><main id="main-content" class="p-reading sk-container sk-stack"><a href="/overview/">Overview</a><h1>KUJO — Trust at the speed of AI.</h1><p>18-slide investor briefing with source evidence, recorded execution and editable planning assumptions.</p><section id="slide-1"><h2><a href="/">1. Trust at the
speed of AI.</a></h2><p>The programming language and accountability infrastructure for agent-driven development.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"></ol><p></p><p>Investor briefing / November 2026</p></section><section id="slide-2"><h2><a href="/2/">2. Software production is outpacing verification.</a></h2><p>Agents can report completion. Teams still need a reviewable chain from requested work to release decision.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>The claim</h3><p>“Tests passed. Ready to merge.” An illustrative agent statement.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>The evidence</h3><p>Command, exit code, changed revision, environment and retained failure output.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>The gap</h3><p>Useful logs already exist. Connecting them across tools and review boundaries remains work.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>02 / The accountability problem</p><p>Thesis · existing agent controls acknowledged</p></section><section id="slide-3"><h2><a href="/3/">3. Greater autonomy requires stronger accountability.</a></h2><p>The cost of an unverified action grows as agents gain authority.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Suggest code</h3><p>Human selects the change → diff review</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Complete a task</h3><p>Agent edits and runs commands → execution records</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Operate a workflow</h3><p>Retries and external effects → boundaries, recovery and approval</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>03 / Structural shift</p><p>Conceptual progression; not a measured adoption curve</p></section><section id="slide-4"><h2><a href="/4/">4. AI use is broad. Trust remains conditional.</a></h2><p>Adoption of AI assistance is not evidence of autonomous production deployment.</p><dl class="p-metrics"><div class="p-metric"><dt>use coding assistants or coding agents at work</dt><dd>65.9%</dd></div><div class="p-metric"><dt>use AI agents or automated workflows at work</dt><dd>26.2%</dd></div><div class="p-metric"><dt>trust AI when answers are easy to validate</dt><dd>48%</dd></div></dl><ol class="p-features"></ol><p></p><p>04 / Why now</p><p>M03–M04 · Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2026</p></section><section id="slide-5"><h2><a href="/5/">5. Build with clarity. Operate with control.</a></h2><p>The language is the long-term foundation. Existing tools offer an earlier route into engineering workflows.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Clarity</h3><p>Kujo language / Spec / Eval: explicit intent and testable outcomes.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Context</h3><p>MCP / CaseFile / RunLedger: useful inputs, debugging evidence and run records.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Control</h3><p>Dispatch / Workcell / ShipCheck: orchestration, bounded execution and release checks.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>05 / The Kujo system</p><p>T01–T14 · tool boundaries remain distinct</p></section><section id="slide-6"><h2><a href="/6/">6. Accountability spans the whole task.</a></h2><p>Each component owns a specific responsibility. A reviewable workflow composes their artifacts explicitly.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Instruction → Context</h3><p>Spec / PackWrite → MCP and repository context</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Execution → Evaluation</h3><p>Dispatch / Workcell → Eval result artifacts</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Evidence → Review → Release</h3><p>CaseFile / RunLedger → reviewer → ShipCheck gate</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>06 / Lifecycle</p><p>Composition map; not automatic end-to-end enforcement</p></section><section id="slide-7"><h2><a href="/7/">7. The foundation is already being built.</a></h2><p>Inspect the implementation and its limits, then reproduce the smallest useful proof.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Language and VM</h3><p>Kujo 1.9.0 is documented; a local 1.9.0 binary executes the recorded fixture tooling.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Bounded execution</h3><p>Workcell 1.3 documents Docker/Podman limits, explicit exports and receipt verification.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Reviewable artifact</h3><p>This presentation uses the native Kujo builder. Its demo retains a failing command, correction and evaluation.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>07 / Engineering proof</p><p>T01, T03, T08, T14 · release documentation ≠ all HEAD features</p></section><section id="slide-8"><h2><a href="/8/">8. A claim becomes inspectable evidence.</a></h2><p>Recorded local coding fixture: correct a post-money ownership calculation. Inspect what failed, what changed and what passed.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Request</h3><p>Return 20% for $6M raised at $30M post-money.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Execution</h3><p>CaseFile captures real Node assertion failure and corrected execution.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Decision</h3><p>Eval checks the correction. Accept this fixture; no production release.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>08 / Recorded demonstration</p><p>Assistant-authored seeded fixture · CaseFile + Eval · local host</p></section><section id="slide-9"><h2><a href="/9/">9. Adopt the ecosystem before the language.</a></h2><p>Keep application code in its existing language. Introduce accountability at the workflow boundary.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Today: wrap the task</h3><p>Use CaseFile for command evidence and Eval for explicit repository checks.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Then: control execution</h3><p>Adopt Dispatch or Workcell after validating the target environment and runtime pins.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Over time: go deeper</h3><p>Choose Kujo for new agent tooling where native language integration adds value.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>09 / Adoption without migration</p><p>T02–T04, T08 · tools have their own runtime dependencies</p></section><section id="slide-10"><h2><a href="/10/">10. Start with teams already delegating engineering work.</a></h2><p>Proposed beachhead: agent-platform builders shipping repeatable engineering workflows.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Buyer / user</h3><p>Platform engineering lead / engineers operating coding-agent harnesses.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Immediate pain</h3><p>Failed runs are expensive to reconstruct. Review spans commits, logs and agent transcripts.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Purchase hypothesis</h3><p>Pay for retained evidence, controlled execution and team review if they reduce investigation time.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>10 / Initial customer</p><p>Proposed segment; no customer interviews or demand validation</p></section><section id="slide-11"><h2><a href="/11/">11. Open tooling can lead to a commercial operating layer.</a></h2><p>Earn developer trust through useful local tools. Charge for shared operational responsibilities.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Available foundation</h3><p>Open repositories and local developer tooling.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Proposed team service</h3><p>Hosted evidence, retention, shared review and execution capacity.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Proposed enterprise tier</h3><p>Organization policy, deployment controls, support and governance.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>11 / Business model</p><p>Hosted offerings and pricing are proposals, not available SKUs</p></section><section id="slide-12"><h2><a href="/12/">12. A focused entry market can support a meaningful business.</a></h2><p>Illustrative bottom-up model. Organization counts and annual contracts are assumptions to validate.</p><dl class="p-metrics"><div class="p-metric"><dt>initial target organizations × $24K ACV</dt><dd>2,000</dd></div><div class="p-metric"><dt>illustrative annual serviceable revenue pool</dt><dd>$48M</dd></div><div class="p-metric"><dt>100 customers × $24K; milestone scenario, not forecast</dt><dd>$2.4M</dd></div></dl><ol class="p-features"></ol><p></p><p>12 / Market model</p><p>Planning only · editable assumptions in appendix</p></section><section id="slide-13"><h2><a href="/13/">13. Accountability requires more than generating code.</a></h2><p>Existing platforms are strong. Kujo must win on useful evidence and the cost of operating reliable workflows.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Coding agents / GitHub</h3><p>Generation, repository context and review are already integrated into major developer surfaces.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Orchestration / LangGraph</h3><p>Persistence, recovery and human review are established capabilities.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Kujo opportunity</h3><p>Connect explicit contracts, bounded work and inspectable artifacts. Prove adoption before claiming a moat.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>13 / Position and defensibility</p><p>C01–C02, T01–T14 · no exclusivity or benchmark superiority</p></section><section id="slide-14"><h2><a href="/14/">14. Meet developers inside their existing workflows.</a></h2><p>The planned December 2026 open-source campaign should produce repeat usage and customer learning.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Discovery</h3><p>GitHub releases, technical demos, docs and reproducible fixtures → qualified visits.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Activation</h3><p>Agent harness recipes and MCP tooling → first successful evidence run.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Retention → revenue</h3><p>Repeat weekly runs → design-partner discussions → paid pilots and renewal evidence.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>14 / Distribution</p><p>Campaign and partnerships planned; measure cohorts, not raw impressions</p></section><section id="slide-15"><h2><a href="/15/">15. Execution is visible. Demand still needs proof.</a></h2><p>Engineering output, external adoption and commercial traction are different measures.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Engineering output</h3><p>Public repositories, documented releases and the locally reproduced evidence fixture.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>External adoption</h3><p>Verified outside usage, retention and independent integrations: data not supplied.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Commercial traction</h3><p>Design partners, paying customers and revenue: data not supplied. Complete before investor distribution.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>15 / Momentum</p><p>Unavailable is not zero; no invented traction</p></section><section id="slide-16"><h2><a href="/16/">16. Built through sustained open-source engineering.</a></h2><p>Robert DeVore connects open-web product work with a growing language and developer-tooling ecosystem.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"><li><span class="p-feature-number">01</span><div><h3>Background</h3><p>Public biography describes years of WordPress, WooCommerce, plugins and publishing systems.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">02</span><div><h3>Current work</h3><p>Language/runtime infrastructure, orchestration, evaluation, telemetry and evidence workflows.</p></div></li><li><span class="p-feature-number">03</span><div><h3>Team enabled by capital</h3><p>Proposed hires in runtime, execution reliability and developer experience; later customer engineering.</p></div></li></ol><p></p><p>16 / Founder and team</p><p>F01 · first-person source; no invented tenure, team or security credentials</p></section><section id="slide-17"><h2><a href="/17/">17. Capital turns a technical foundation into an adopted platform.</a></h2><p>Working seed scenario. Financing terms and hiring pace remain assumptions.</p><dl class="p-metrics"><div class="p-metric"><dt>target raise</dt><dd>$6M</dd></div><div class="p-metric"><dt>post-money valuation or SAFE cap assumption</dt><dd>$30M</dd></div><div class="p-metric"><dt>planned runway, including reserve</dt><dd>24 mo</dd></div></dl><ol class="p-features"></ol><p></p><p>17 / Investment and milestones</p><p>20% simplified ownership before further dilution · $150K founder annual base</p></section><section id="slide-18"><h2><a href="/18/">18. Agents will build more software.</a></h2><p>The organizations deploying it will still be accountable for what it does.</p><dl class="p-metrics"></dl><ol class="p-features"></ol><p></p><p>18 / The vision</p><p>KUJO · Trust at the speed of AI.</p></section></main><script type="module" src="https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js/vce701823e78c4301a35b7e07294d87301792088814092" integrity="sha512-UKNk8GbxkABxKcXYuLkO16EleLGUkcvJWtpSNmExE0Too3ZaFAxTscNWEc1OdAv5/BikHawapSCaiD2XCmp+Bw==" data-cf-beacon='{"version":"2024.11.0","token":"1d30a17a0e01472db240809ad42c0b1f","r":1,"spa":2}' crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
</body></html>